course: dictionary
lesson: 5715
title: The loo and other everyday words
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The loo is an everyday word for the toilet, or the room with the toilet. You will hear it most in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand. «The loo» is the everyday British word for the toilet, so if someone asks where the loo is, they want the toilet.
explain: Use the context to check. «She smiles in every photo, then cries in the loo, happy and sad at once.» People usually go to the loo to be alone and private, so the loo is a room you go into by yourself. Words like this are friendly and everyday, not formal.
explain: Be careful: loo sounds like the French «le loup», which means wolf, not toilet. The French word is said "LOO" too, but it is a different word. When two words sound the same, look at the sentence to see which meaning fits.
fact: Loo is an informal word, so it is friendly and everyday rather than formal.
fact: Loo means the toilet, or the room with the toilet.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, people say loo; Americans usually say bathroom or restroom.
fact: Phrases to know: go to the loo, need the loo, loo roll (toilet paper), loo break.
fact: «Le loup» is French for wolf, said "LOO"; it is not the same word as loo.
exercise: «The loo» is the everyday British word for the toilet. What does loo mean? | The kitchen sink | *The toilet, or the room with the toilet | A kind of wolf | A bedroom
exercise: Priya from Dublin says, «I need the loo, back in a minute.» Where is she going? | To bed | To the shops | To the park | *To the toilet
exercise: Le loup is French for wolf, said "LOO". Is it the same as the English word loo? | Yes, it means a bear | No, it means a fox | *No, it sounds the same but means a wolf | Yes, it means the toilet
test: «She cries in the loo, happy and sad at once.» Why would someone go to the loo to cry? | To feed the dog | *To be alone and private | To cook dinner | To sing with friends
test: Which phrase means toilet paper in British English? | Go to the loo | Loo break | Need the loo | *Loo roll
exam: Which sentence uses loo correctly? | The loo is a kind of bird that sings at night. | Americans say loo for the bedroom. | *Sorry, I need the loo, could you wait a minute? | Le loo is the wolf in the forest.
